2991,
2992,"Narrated Ibn 'Abbas:""When this Ayah was revealed: And whether you disclose what is in yourselves or conceal it"
2993,"Narrated 'Aishah:""I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about Allah's saying: So"
2994,"Narrated 'Aishah:""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked about this Ayah: 'It is He who sent down to you the Book. In it are Ayat that are entirely clear... (3:7)' until the end of the Ayah. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'When you see those who seek what is not entirely clear thereof"
2995,"Narrated 'Abdullah:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Every Prophet had a Wali among the Prophets. My Wali is my father Ibrahim the Khalil of my Lord."" Then he recited: Verily among mankind who have the best claim to Ibrahim"
2996,"Narrated 'Abdullah:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever takes a false oath to deprive a Muslim of his property"
2997,"Narrated Anas:""When this Ayah was revealed: By no means shall you attain Al-Birr unless you spend of that which you love (3:92). Or"
2998,"Narrated Ibn 'Umar:""A man stood before the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'Who is the (real) Hajj pilgrim"
2999,"Narrated 'Amir bin Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas:from his father who said: ""When this Ayah was revealed: 'Come"
3000,"Narrated Abu Ghalib:""Abu Umamah saw heads (of the Khawarij) hanging on the streets of Damascus. He said: 'The dogs of the Fire and the worst dead people under the canopy of the heavens. The best dead men are those whom these have killed.' He then recited: On the Day when some faces will become white and some faces will become black... (3:106) until the end of the Ayah. I said to Abu Umamah: 'Did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?' He said: 'If I had not heard it but one time"
3001,"Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, saying: Wakīʿ narrated to us, saying: Abū Farwah Yazīd ibn Sinān narrated to us, from al-Mubārak, from Ṣuhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""He has not believed in the Quran who makes lawful its prohibitions.""

Abū ʿĪsā said: This hadith is not strong in its chain of narration, and Wakīʿ was contradicted in his narration. Muḥammad said: Abū Farwah Yazīd ibn Sinān al-Ruhāwī is not blameworthy in his narrations, except for the narrations of his son Muḥammad from him, for he narrates rejected reports from him.

Abū ʿĪsā said: Muḥammad ibn Yazīd ibn Sinān narrated this hadith from his father and added to this chain: from Mujāhid, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Ṣuhayb. Muḥammad ibn Yazīd is not followed in his narration, and he is weak. And al-Mubārak is an unknown narrator."
3002,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah, who said:** Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Bahir ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Kathir ibn Murrah al-Hadrami, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, who said:

""I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say:

**'The one who recites the Qur'an openly is like the one who gives charity openly, and the one who recites the Qur'an in secret is like the one who gives charity in secret.'**""

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: **""This is a hasan gharib hadith.""**

**The meaning of this hadith** is that the one who recites the Qur'an in secret is superior to the one who recites the Qur'an openly, because the secret charity is considered superior by the people of knowledge to the open charity. The intent of this, according to the people of knowledge, is so that a man may be safeguarded from self-admiration (*'ujb*), for the one who conceals his action is not feared to fall into self-admiration to the same degree as one whose action is performed openly.

---

*Reported by Imam Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi, who graded it as **Hasan Gharib**.*"
