3229,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Abd ibn Humayd narrated to us:** Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'eed ibn Jubayr — and he did not mention Ibn 'Abbas — and from Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'eed ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, regarding the Words of Allah, the Most High:

> **""And do not recite your prayer aloud""** (Surah Al-Isra, 17:108)

He said: ""This verse was revealed in Makkah. Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his voice with the recitation of the Qur'an, the polytheists would revile the One Who revealed it and the one who brought it. So Allah, the Most High, revealed:

> **'And do not recite your prayer aloud'** — lest they revile the Qur'an, the One Who revealed it, and the one who brought it —

> **'Nor speak it too softly'** — so that your Companions may not hear it, that they may learn the Qur'an from you.""

Abu 'Eesaa (at-Tirmidhi) said: **""This is a hasan saheeh hadith.""**

---

*Note: The chain of narration (isnad) is graded as hasan saheeh by Imam at-Tirmidhi (رحمه"
3230,"Narrated Samurah:that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Sam was the father of Arabs"
3231,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ibn Abi 'Umar, who narrated from Sufyan, from Mis'ar, from 'Asim ibn Abi an-Najud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said:**

I said to **Hudhayfah ibn al-Yamān** (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray in **Bayt al-Maqdis** (Jerusalem)?""

He said: **""No.""**

I said: **""Indeed, he did!""**

He said: **""Do you say that, O bald one? By what do you say that?""**

I said: **""By the Qur'an — between you and me is the Qur'an.""**

So **Hudhayfah** (may Allah be pleased with him) said: **""Whoever argues by the Qur'an has indeed succeeded.""**

Sufyan said: He (Hudhayfah) said: **""He has indeed argued (successfully).""** And sometimes he said: **""He has indeed prevailed.""**

Then he recited:

> **""Glorified is He Who took His servant by night from al-Masjid al-Haram to al-Masjid al-Aqsa.""** *(Surah al-Isra', 17:1)*

He (Hudhayfah) said: **""Do you think he prayed in it?""**

I said: **""No.""**

He said: **""Had he prayed in it, prayer would have been prescribed for you therein, just as prayer was prescribed for you in al-Masjid al-Haram.""**

**Hudhayfah** (may Allah be pleased with him) said: **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought a creature with a long back, extended thus — its stride reaching as far as the eye could see. They did not leave the back of al-Buraq until they saw Paradise, Hellfire, and all of the Hereafter's promises. Then they returned as they had departed.""**

He said: **""And they narrate that he tied it up — why? Lest it flee from him. But rather, it was subdued for him by the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed.""**

**Abu 'Isa (at-Tirmidhi)** said: **""This is a hasan sahih hadith.""**

---

*And Allah, the Most High, knows best.*"
3232,
3233,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ibn Abi 'Umar, who narrated from Sufyan, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said:**

I said to Ibn 'Abbas, ""Indeed, an-Nawf al-Bikali claims that Musa (Moses), the companion of the Children of Israel, is not the same as Musa, the companion of al-Khadir."" He said, ""The enemy of Allah has lied! I heard Ubayy ibn Ka'b say that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say:

**'Musa stood as an orator among the Children of Israel and was asked, ""Who among the people is the most knowledgeable?"" He said, ""I am the most knowledgeable."" So Allah rebuked him for not referring the knowledge back to Him. Then Allah revealed to him: ""There is a servant of Mine at the junction of the two seas who is more knowledgeable than you.""**

**Musa said, ""O my Lord, how can I find him?"" He was told, ""Carry a fish in a basket, and wherever you lose the fish, that is where he will be."" So Musa set out, and his young companion went with him — he was Yusha' ibn Nun (Joshua, son of Nun) — and it is also said Yu'sa. Musa carried a fish in a basket, and he and his young companion set out walking until they reached a rock. There, Musa and his young companion lay down to rest. The fish stirred in the basket until it escaped from the basket and fell into the sea. Allah held back the flow of water around it so that it formed an arch-like passage. Thus the fish had a tunnel through the water, and for Musa and his young companion it was a marvel.**

**They continued on for the remainder of that day and that night. Musa's companion forgot to inform him of what had happened. When morning came, Musa said to his young companion:**

***""Bring us our morning food; indeed, from this journey of ours we have found fatigue.""***

**He had not become fatigued until he had passed the place where he had been commanded to go. The young companion said:**

***""Do you recall when we took refuge at the rock? Indeed, I forgot the fish — and none made me forget to mention it except Satan — and it took its course into the sea in a wondrous manner.""***

**Musa said:**

***""That is what we were seeking!"" So they retraced their footsteps, following their tracks back.""***

**Sufyan said: ""Some people claim that at that rock there is the Spring of Life, and that no dead thing touched by its water but that it would come back to life. The fish had been partially eaten from, and when water dripped upon it, it came back to life.""**

**They retraced their tracks until they came to the rock, where they saw a man lying covered with a garment. Musa greeted him with salam (peace). He said, ""How is it that there is salam in your land?"" He said, ""I am Musa."" He said, ""Musa of the Children of Israel?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""O Musa, indeed you possess knowledge from the knowledge of Allah which Allah has taught you and which I do not know, and I possess knowledge from the knowledge of Allah which He has taught me and which you do not know.""**

**Musa said:**

***""May I follow you on the condition that you teach me from what you have been taught of right guidance?""***

**He (al-Khadir) said:**

***""Indeed, you will not be able to have patience with me. And how can you have patience regarding that of which you do not have comprehensive knowledge?""***

**Musa said:**

***""You will find me, if Allah wills, patient, and I will not disobey you in any matter.""***

**Al-Khadir said:**

***""Then if you follow me, do not ask me about anything until I myself mention it to you.""***

**He said, ""Yes."" So al-Khadir and Musa set out walking along the shore of the sea. A ship passed by them, and they spoke to the people of the ship asking them to take them aboard. They recognized al-Khadir and carried them aboard without charge. Al-Khadir then went to one of the planks of the ship and pulled it out. Musa said to him, ""These people carried us without charge, and yet you have gone and scuttled their ship —**

***to drown its people! Indeed, you have done a terrible thing!""***

**He said:**

***""Did I not tell you that you would not be able to have patience with me?""***

**Musa said:**

***""Do not take me to task for what I forgot, and do not make my affair difficult for me.""***

**Then they disembarked from the ship. While they were walking along the shore, a young boy was playing with other boys. Al-Khadir took hold of his head and tore it off with his hand, killing him. Musa said to him:**

***""Have you killed an innocent soul for no soul? Indeed, you have done a monstrous thing!""***

**He said:**

***""Did I not tell you that you would not be able to have patience with me?""***

**Musa said, ""This is more serious than the first."" He said:**

***""If I ask you about anything after this, then do not keep me as your companion. You have already received an excuse from me.""***

**So they set out until they came to the people of a village and asked its people for food, but they refused to offer them hospitality. There they found a wall that was about to collapse"" — meaning it was leaning — ""and al-Khadir set it upright with his hand:**

***""He straightened it.""***

**Musa said to him, ""We came to these people and they neither hosted us nor fed us —**

***if you wished, you could have taken a wage for it.""***

**He said:**

***""This is the parting between me and you. I will inform you of the interpretation of that over which you could not bear patience.""***

**The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings upon him, said:**

***""May Allah have mercy on Musa! We wish he had been patient so that he could have related to us more of their story.""***

**And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings upon him, said:**

***""The first instance was due to Musa's forgetfulness.""***

**Then a sparrow came and perched on the edge of the ship and then pecked at the sea. Al-Khadir said to Musa, ""My knowledge and your knowledge have not decreased the knowledge of Allah except as much as this sparrow has decreased the sea.""**

**Sa'id ibn Jubayr said: ""And Ibn 'Abbas used to recite: 'And before them there was a king who would seize every sound ship by force.' And he used to recite: 'And as for the boy, he was a disbeliever.'""**

**Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: ""This is a hadith that is hasan sahih (good and sound).""**

**It was also narrated by al-Zuhri from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah from Ibn 'Abbas from Ubayy ibn Ka'b from the Prophet, peace and blessings upon him. And it was also narrated by Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas from Ubayy ibn Ka'b from the Prophet, peace and blessings upon him.**

**Abu 'Isa said: ""I heard Abu Muzahim al-Samarqandi say: 'I heard Ali ibn al-Madini say: ""I performed Hajj one year, and I had no ambition except to hear from Sufyan the narration of this hadith, until I heard him say: 'Narrated to us 'Amr ibn Dinar.' I had heard this hadith from Sufyan before that, but he had not mentioned the narration (khabar) in it.""'**

---

*This is the well-known hadith of Musa and al-Khadir, recorded in the Jami' of Imam Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi, and it is also found in the two Sahih collections of al-Bukhari and Muslim. It recounts the journey of Musa, peace be upon him, in search of al-Khadir, and the three incidents that tested Musa's patience — the scuttling of the ship, the killing of the young boy, and the rebuilding of the wall — each of which carried hidden wisdom known only to Allah, the Most High.*"
3234,"Narrated Mu'adh bin Jabal [may Allah be pleased with him]:""One morning"
3235,"Narrated 'Abdullah bin Az-Zubair:from his father who said: ""When (the following) was revealed: 'Then"
3236,"Narrated Asma bint Yazid:""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reciting: 'Say: ""O My slaves who have transgressed against themselves! Despair not of the mercy of Allah"
3237,"Narrated 'Abdullah:""A Jew came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'O Muhammad! Allah will seize the heavens upon a finger"
3238,"Narrated 'Abdullah:""So the Prophet (ﷺ) laughed in amazement and approval"
3239,"Abū Sa‘īd al-Ashajj and Abū Mūsā Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, both saying: Ibn Idrīs narrated to us, from his father, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from ‘Alqamah ibn Wā’il, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shu‘bah (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) sent me to Najrān. They said to me: “Do you not recite [in your Book]: ‘O sister of Hārūn’ (Maryam 19:28), yet there was [a great span of time] between ‘Īsā (Jesus, peace be upon him) and Mūsā (Moses, peace be upon him)?” I did not know how to answer them. So I returned to the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him. He said: “Why did you not inform them that they used to name their children after their prophets and the righteous who came before them?”

Abū ‘Īsā [al-Tirmidhī] said: This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ gharīb hadith; we do not know it except through the narration of Ibn Idrīs."
3240,"Narrated Mujahid:that Ibn 'Abbas said: ""Do you know what is the width of Jahannam?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Yes"
3241,"Narrated Masruq:that 'Aishah said: ""O Messenger of Allah! - On the Day of Resurrection the whole earth will be grasped by His Hand and the heavens will be rolled up in His Right Hand (39:67). Where will the believers be?"" He said: ""Upon the Sirat O 'Aishah"
3242,"Narrated Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""How can I be comfortable when the one with the horn is holding it in his lips and his forehead is leaning forward"
3243,"Narrated 'Abdullah bin 'Amr [may Allah be pleased with him]:""A Bedouin said: 'O Messenger of Allah! What is As-Sur?' He said: 'A horn which is blown into"
3244,"Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from al-Suddī, from Murrah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, regarding the verse: *""And there is none among you but will pass over it [Hell]""* (Qurʾān 19:71). He said: ""They will pass over it, then they will depart according to their deeds.""

Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from Shuʿbah, from al-Suddī, with the same meaning.

ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I said to Shuʿbah, ""Isrāʾīl narrated to me from al-Suddī, from Murrah, from ʿAbdullāh, from the Prophet ﷺ."" Shuʿbah said: ""I heard it from al-Suddī as a marfūʿ narration, but I deliberately abandoned it."""
3245,"Narrated Abu Hurairah:that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""A caller will call out: 'You shall have life and never die; you shall be healthy and never be ill; you shall be young and never grow old; you shall live in favor and never suffer difficult circumstances.' That is the saying of Allah Most High: This is Paradise"
3246,"Narrated An-Nu'man bin Bashir:that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Supplication is the worship."" Then he recited: 'And your Lord said: 'Call upon Me"
3247,"# Hadith Translation

**Narrated by Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him):**

When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) returned from Khaybar, he traveled through the night until drowsiness overcame him. He then dismounted and rested for the night. He (ﷺ) said, ""O Bilal, keep watch for us during the night.""

Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) then performed prayer, after which he leaned against his mount, facing the break of dawn. Thereupon, his eyes overcame him and he fell asleep. None of them awoke. The first among them to awaken was the Prophet (ﷺ). He (ﷺ) called out, ""O Bilal!"" Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) replied, ""May my father be your ransom, O Messenger of Allah! The same One who took hold of your soul has taken hold of my soul.""

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then said, ""Lead forth (the camels)."" He (ﷺ) then dismounted, performed ablution, called the iqamah for prayer, and then offered a prayer similar to his (usual) prayer for its prescribed time, with deliberation and composure. He (ﷺ) then recited: **""And establish prayer for My remembrance""** (Ṭāhā, 20:14).

---

**Regarding its chain of narration:**

This hadith is not well-preserved (ghayr mahfūẓ). A number of huffāẓ (hadith scholars) have narrated it from al-Zuhrī from Sa'īd ibn al-Musayyab, stating that the Prophet (ﷺ) [offered the prayer] — but they did not mention therein a narration from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him).

Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar is considered weak (yuḍa''af) in hadith. Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd al-Qaṭṭān and others have deemed him weak on account of his memory (ḥifẓ)."
3248,"Narrated 'Abdullah:""I was hiding beneath the covering of the Ka'bah"
3249,"Narrated Anas bin Malik:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited: Verily those who say: ""Our Lord is Allah"
3250,"Narrated Tawus:Ibn 'Abbas was asked about this Ayah: Say: ""No reward do I ask of you for this except to be kind for my kinship with you (42:23)."" So Sa'eed bin Jubair said: 'To be kind to the family of Muhammad.' Ibn 'Abbas replied: 'You know that there was no family of the Quraish except that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had some relatives among them.' He said: 'Except that you should uphold ties of kinship that exist between me and you"
3251,"Narrated 'Ubaidullah bin Al-Wazi':""A Shaikh from Banu Murrah narrated to me"
3252,"Narrated Abu Umamah:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""No people go astray after having been guided"
3253,"Narrated Masruq:""A man came to 'Abdullah and said: 'A story teller has said that a smoke will appear from the earth"
3254,"Narrated Anas bin Malik:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no believer except that he has two doors: A door through which his deeds ascend"
3255,"Narrated 'Abdul-Malik bin 'Umair:from the nephew of 'Abdullah bin Salam who said: ""When they were after 'Uthman"
3256,"Narrated 'Aishah [may Allah be pleased with her]:""When the Prophet (ﷺ) saw storm clouds he would pace back and forth. And when it rained"
3257,
3258,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Abd ibn Humayd narrated to us:** Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allāh be pleased with him), that **al-Rubayyi' bint al-Naḍr** came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), whose son **Ḥārithah ibn Surāqah** had been struck on the Day of Badr by a stray arrow. She came to the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) and said:

*""Inform me about Ḥārithah. If he has attained good, I shall be patient and seek reward; but if he has not attained good, I shall exert myself in supplication (for him).""*

The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

*""O Umm Ḥārithah, verily there are many gardens within Paradise, and your son has attained **al-Firdaws al-A'lā** (the Highest Firdaws).""* And al-Firdaws is the **highest elevation, the center, and the most excellent part of Paradise.**

He (the compiler) said: *""This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīh gharīb Ḥadīth from the narrations of Anas (may Allāh be pleased with him).""*"
3259,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ibn Abi 'Umar, who narrated from Sufyan, who narrated from Malik ibn Mihwal, from 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn Sa'id ibn Wahb al-Hamdani, that 'Ā'ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said:**

""I asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ about this verse: *'And those who give what they give while their hearts are fearful'* (Sūrah al-Mu'minūn, 23:60).""

'Ā'ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) said: ""Are they those who drink wine and steal?""

He ﷺ replied: **""No, O daughter of the Truthful One (al-Ṣiddīq). Rather, they are those who fast, and pray, and give charity, yet they fear that their deeds may not be accepted from them. It is they who hasten toward good deeds, and they are the foremost in attaining them.""**

It is also reported that this Hadith has been narrated through another chain from 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn Sa'id, from Abū Ḥāzim, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ, with a similar meaning.

---

**Reference:** This Hadith is recorded in *Sunan al-Tirmidhī* (Hadith No. 3175), classified as **Ḥasan Gharīb** by al-Tirmidhī."
3260,"Narrated Abu Hurairah:""Some people among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Who are these people whom Allah mentioned"
3261,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Abd ibn Humayd narrated to us:** Rawh ibn 'Ubaydah narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Akhnas, who informed me: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said:

There was a man called **Marthad ibn Abi Marthad**, who used to carry captives from Makkah to bring them to al-Madinah. He said: There was a prostitute in Makkah called **'Anaq**, who was a companion of his. He had promised one of the captives of Makkah that he would carry him (to safety).

He said: I came until I reached the shade of one of the walls of Makkah on a moonlit night. He said: Then 'Anaq came and saw the darkness of my shadow beside the wall. When she reached me, she recognized it and said, ""Marthad?"" I said, ""Marthad."" She said, ""Welcome! Come, stay with us tonight.""

He said: I said, ""O 'Anaq, Allah has forbidden fornication (al-zina).""

She said, ""O people of the tents! This man is carrying your captives!""

He said: Then eight men followed me, and I took the route of al-Khandaq. I reached a cave or a grotto and entered it. They came and stood over my head and urinated, and their urine dripped upon my head, but Allah blinded them from seeing me.

He said: Then they departed, and I returned to my companion and carried him. He was a heavy man, until I reached the place of al-Idhkhir grass. I then unfettered his shackles, and I carried him, and he assisted me, until I arrived in al-Madinah.

I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, may I marry 'Anaq?"" — twice. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, remained silent and did not reply to me with anything, until the verse was revealed:

> *""The fornicator shall not marry save a fornicatress or an idolatress, and the fornicatress — none shall marry her save a fornicator or an idolater; and that is forbidden to the believers.""* (An-Nur, 24:3)

Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: **""O Marthad, the fornicator shall not marry save a fornicatress or an idolatress, and the fornicatress — none shall marry her save a fornicator or an idolater. So do not marry her.""**

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a **hasan gharib** hadith. We do not know it except from this chain of narration."
3262,"Narrated Anas [May Allah be pleased with him]:""While the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was returning from Al-Hudaibiyyah it was revealed to him"
3263,Narrated Anas:that eighty people swooped down from the mountain of At-Tan'im to kill the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during Salat As-Subh
3264,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her):**

She said: ""When that which was said about me was mentioned — and I was unaware of it — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood up among the people to address them. He pronounced the *tashahhud* and praised Allah and extolled Him with that which He is worthy. Then he said: **'Now then: advise me regarding certain people who have slandered my household. By Allah, I know of no evil upon my household whatsoever. And they have slandered a man — by Allah, I know of no evil upon him whatsoever. He never entered my house except in my presence, and he never departed on a journey except that he departed with me.'**

Then Saʿd ibn Muʿāḍh (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and said: 'Permit me, O Messenger of Allah, to strike their necks.' Then a man from the Khazraj stood — and the mother of Ḥassān ibn Thābit was from the clan of that man — and said: 'You have lied! By Allah, had they been from the Aws, you would not have liked their necks to be struck.' It was nearly the case that a conflict arose between the Aws and the Khazraj in the mosque, and I was unaware of it.

When the evening of that day came, I went out for some need of mine, accompanied by Umm Misṭaḥ. She stumbled and said: 'May Misṭaḥ be ruined!' I said to her: 'What! Do you curse your son?' She fell silent. Then she stumbled a second time and said: 'May Misṭaḥ be ruined!' I said to her: 'What! Do you curse your son?' She fell silent. Then she stumbled a third time and said: 'May Misṭaḥ be ruined!' So I rebuked her and said: 'What! Do you curse your son?' She said: 'By Allah, I only curse him because of you.' I said: 'Because of what?' So she disclosed the entire account to me. I said: 'Has this really happened?' She said: 'Yes.' By Allah, I returned to my house as though I had not gone out for anything at all — I found neither little nor much relief from it. I fell ill, so I said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): 'Send me to my father's house.' So he sent the young servant with me. I entered the house and found Umm Rūmān below, and Abū Bakr above the house, reciting. My mother said: 'What brings you here, my daughter?' She said: 'So I informed her and recounted the account to her, but it had not affected her as it had affected me.' She said: 'My daughter, lighten the matter upon yourself, for by Allah, rarely is a beautiful woman with a man who loves her, and who has co-wives, except that they envy her and speak against her.' She said: 'But it had not affected her as it had affected me.' She said: 'I said: ""Has my father come to know of it?"" She said: ""Yes."" I said: ""And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?"" She said: ""Yes.""' She said: 'I broke down and wept. Abū Bakr heard my voice while he was above the house reciting, so he came down and said to my mother: ""What is the matter with her?"" She said: ""She has learned of that which has been said about her."" His eyes overflowed with tears and he said: ""I adjure you, my daughter, return to your house."" So I returned.

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had come to my house and asked my servant-girl about me. She said: 'By Allah, I know of no fault in her, except that she would sleep until the sheep would enter and eat her leavened dough or her batter.' Some of his Companions rebuked her and said: 'Speak truthfully to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).' So they caused her to stumble in her words. She said: 'Glory be to Allah! By Allah, I know of no fault in her except what a goldsmith knows about a piece of red gold.' The matter reached the man about whom it had been said, and he said: 'Glory be to Allah! By Allah, I have never uncovered the veil of any woman.' ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: 'He was killed as a martyr in the cause of Allah.'

She said: 'My parents came to me in the morning and remained with me until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came to me after he had prayed ʿAṣr. He entered while my parents flanked me, one on my right and one on my left. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) pronounced the *tashahhud*, praised Allah, and extolled Him with that which He is worthy. Then he said: **""Now then, O ʿĀʾishah, if you have committed an injustice or transgressed, then repent to Allah, for Allah accepts repentance from His servants.""**

She said: 'A woman from the Anṣār was sitting at the door, and I said: ""Does this woman not feel shy to mention anything?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) admonished, so I turned to my father and said: ""Answer him."" He said: ""What shall I say?"" I turned to my mother and said: ""Answer her."" She said: ""What shall I say?"" When they did not respond, I pronounced the *tashahhud*, praised Allah, and extolled Him with that which He is worthy. Then I said: **""By Allah, if I tell you that I did not do it — and Allah bears witness that I am truthful — that will not benefit me at all with you, for you have spoken of it and your hearts have imbibed it. And if I tell you that I did it — and Allah knows that I did not do it — you will say: 'She has confessed it against herself.' And by Allah, I find for myself and for you no example""** — she said: 'I sought the name of Yaʿqūb (peace be upon him) but could not recall it, except as Abū Yūsuf, when he said: ***""So [the most fitting response is] beautiful patience, and Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe""*** (Yūsuf, 12:18).'

She said: 'Revelation descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) at that very moment, and we fell silent. It was lifted from him, and I could discern the joy upon his face as he wiped his forehead and said: **""Good tidings, O ʿĀʾishah! Allah has revealed your innocence.""**

She said: 'I was at the height of my anger, so my parents said to me: ""Rise and go to him."" I said: ""No, by Allah! I will not rise to him, nor praise him, nor praise you both. Rather, I praise Allah, Who has revealed my innocence. You have heard it, yet you neither denied it nor changed it.""

ʿĿʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to say: 'As for Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, Allah protected her through her religion, so she said nothing but good. As for her sister Ḥamnah, she was destroyed among those who were destroyed. Those who spoke about it were Misṭaḥ, Ḥassān ibn Thābit, and the hypocrite ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl — and he was the one who managed and gathered it, and he was the one who bore the greatest burden of it among them — he and Ḥamnah.'

She said: 'Abū Bakr swore that he would never bestow any benefit upon Misṭaḥ again. So Allah, the Most High, revealed this verse: ***""And let not those of virtue among you and wealth swear not to give [aid] to their relatives, the needy, and the emigrants for the cause of Allah""*** (An-Nūr, 24:22) — referring to Abū Bakr — ***""and let them pardon and overlook. Would you not like that Allah should forgive you? And Allah is Forgiving and Merciful""*** (An-Nūr, 24:22) — referring to Misṭaḥ.

Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Yes, by Allah, our Lord! Indeed, we love that You forgive us.' And he resumed doing for Misṭaḥ what he had previously done for him.

Abū ʿĪsā said: 'This is a *ḥasan ṣaḥīh gharīb* hadith from the hadith of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. It has also been narrated by Yūnus ibn Yazīd, Maʿmar, and others from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, ʿAlqamah ibn Wāqqāṣ al-Laythī, and ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh, from ʿĀʾishah — this hadith being longer and more complete than the hadith of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.'""

---

*This is the hadith of the Slander (al-Ifk), one of the most significant narrations in Islamic history, recorded in the collections of al-Tirmidhī and others, concerning the false accusation against the Mother of the Believers, ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and her subsequent vindication by divine revelation.*"
3265,"Narrated 'Abdullah bin Az-Zubair:""Al-Aqra' bin Habis arrived to meet the Prophet (ﷺ)"" - he said - ""so Abu Bakr said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Appoint him over his people.' 'Umar said: 'Do not appoint him O Messenger of Allah!' They continued talking before the Prophet (ﷺ) until they raised their voices. Abu Bakr said to 'Umar: 'You only wanted to contradict me.' So ['Umar] said: 'I did not want to contradict you.'"" He said: ""So this Ayah was revealed: 'O you who believe! Do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet (49:2).'"" He said: ""After that"
3266,Narrated Al-Bara bin 'Azib:regarding the saying of Allah the Most High: Verily
3267,"Narrated Abu Jubairah bin Ad-Dahhak:""A man among us would be known by two or three names. He would be called by one that perhaps he disliked"
3268,"Narrated Abu Nadrah:""Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri recited: And know that among you there is the Messenger of Allah. If he were to obey you in much of the matter"
3269,Narrated Ibn 'Umar:that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave a Khutbah to the people on the day of the conquest of Makkah
3270,"Narrated Al-Hasan:from Samurah that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Al-Hasab is wealth and Al-Karam is Taqwa"
3271,"Narrated Anas bin Malik:that Allah's Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Jahannam will continue saying: 'Are there any more' until the Might Lord puts His Foot over it. It will say: 'Enough! Enough! By Your Might.' And one side of it will close in on the other"
3272,"Narrated Abu Wa'il:from a man of Rabi'ah who said: ""I arrived in Al-Madinah"
3273,"Narrated Abu Wa'il:that Al-Harith bin Yazid Al-Bakri said: ""I arrived in Al-Madinah and entered the Masjid and found it full with the people and I also noticed a black banner raised high"
3274,"Narrated Ibn 'Umar:that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""And at the setting of the stars (52:49) (about) the two Rak'ah before Fajr. And after the prostrations (50:40) 'The two Rak'at after Al-Maghrib"
3275,"Narrated ['Abdullah] bin Mas'ud:""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reached Sidrat Al-Muntaha"" He said: 'There terminates everything that ascends from the earth"
3276,"Narrated Ash-Shaibani:""I asked Zirr bin Hubaish about the saying of Allah the Might and Sublime: And was a distance of two bow lengths or less (53:9). So he said: 'Ibn Mas'ud informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw Jibra'il"
3277,"Narrated by al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, who narrated from Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi 'Amrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding the Words of Allah, the Most High:

**""Alif, Lam, Mim. The Romans have been defeated in the nearest land...""**

He (Ibn 'Abbas) said: They were defeated, and then they prevailed. The polytheists loved for the Persians to triumph over the Romans, for both they and the Persians were people of idols. But the Muslims loved for the Romans to triumph over the Persians, for the Romans were People of the Book.

They mentioned this to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abu Bakr mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: **""Indeed, they (the Romans) will prevail.""**

Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) then told them of this, and they said: ""Set a term between us and you; if we prevail, such-and-such will be ours, and if you prevail, such-and-such will be yours."" So he set a term of five years. But they did not prevail within that time.

They mentioned this to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: **""Why did you not set it to less than—""** (the narrator said: I think he said) **""ten.""**

Sa'id said: ""And 'bid' (a few) refers to less than ten.""

He said: Then the Romans prevailed thereafter.

He said: That is the meaning of the Words of the Most High:

**""Alif, Lam, Mim. The Romans have been defeated...""** up to His Words: **""And the believers will rejoice in the victory of Allah. He gives victory to whom He wills.""**

Sufyan said: I heard that they (the Romans) prevailed over them on the Day of Badr.

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a hasan sahih gharib hadith. We only know it from the hadith of Sufyan al-Thawri from Habib ibn Abi 'Amrah."
