3305,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Abd ibn Humayd narrated to us:** Rawh ibn 'Ubādah narrated to us, from 'Awf, from al-Hasan, Muhammad, and Khilās, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said:

> Indeed, Mūsā (peace be upon him) was a man of great modesty and concealment, such that none of his skin was ever seen, out of his bashfulness. Thus, those among the Children of Israel who wished to harm him said: ""He conceals himself with this covering only because of some defect in his skin — either leprosy, or a hernia, or some other affliction."" And indeed, Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, desired to clear him of what they had said.
>
> Now, Mūsā (peace be upon him) one day went off alone. He placed his garments upon a stone and then bathed. When he had finished, he turned toward his garments to take them, but the stone carried off his garment. So Mūsā took his staff and pursued the stone, saying: ""My garment, O stone! My garment, O stone!"" — until he reached a gathering of the Children of Israel. They saw him naked, in the most beautiful form of any man, and Allāh cleared him of what they had been saying.
>
> Then the stone stood still, and he took his garment, put it on, and began striking the stone with his staff. By Allāh, there remained upon the stone marks from the blows of his staff — three, four, or five. And that is the meaning of the Most High's words:
>
> **""O you who believe! Be not like those who abused Mūsā; but Allāh cleared him of what they said, and he was honourable in the sight of Allāh.""** (Sūrat al-Aḥzāb, 33:69)

Abū 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith.** It has been narrated through other chains from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and there is also a narration from Anas (may Allāh be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."
