3389,Thawban (ra) said:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ‘Whoever says when he reaches the evening: “I am pleased with Allah as (my) Lord
3390,"Narrated by Abd ibn Humayd, narrated by Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would not test [the believing women] except with the verse in which Allah, the Exalted, says: 'When the believing women come to you to pledge allegiance to you...' (Surah al-Mumtahinah, 60:12)."" Ma'mar said: ""Ibn Tawus informed me from his father, who said: 'The hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) never touched the hand of any woman except a woman over whom he had authority [i.e., a wife or a female relative within the prohibited degrees].'"" Abu Isa [al-Tirmidhi] said: ""This is a hasan sahih hadith."""
3391,Abu Hurairah (ra) said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach his Companions
3392,"Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated from al-Agharr ibn al-Sabbah, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, from Abu Nasr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the Words of Allah, the Most High: ""O you who believe, when believing women come to you as emigrants, examine them..."" He (Ibn Abbas) said: When a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) to embrace Islam, he would make her swear by Allah: ""I did not leave out of hatred for my husband; I left only out of love for Allah and His Messenger."" Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a gharib (uncommon) hadith."
3393,Shaddad bin Aws narrated that:The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: “Should I not direct you to the chief of supplications for forgiveness? ‘O Allah
3394,Al-Bara' bin `Azib narrated that:The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: “Should I not teach you some words to say when you go to your bed
3395,Rafi` bin Khadij narrated that:The Prophet said: “When one of you lies down on his right side
3396,"# Translation of the Hadith

Abd ibn Humaid narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Arqam, who said:

""I was with my uncle when I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul say to his companions: **'Do not spend upon those who are with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they disperse,'** and **'If we return to Madinah, the most honourable among us will expel therefrom the most abominable.'**

I mentioned that to my uncle, and my uncle mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ then called for me, and I informed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Abdullah ibn Ubayy and his companions, and they swore that they had not said it. Thus, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared me a liar and affirmed his word. There befell me a distress such as had never befallen me before, so I remained seated in my house.

My uncle said: 'You only intended that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would declare you a liar and detest you.'

Then Allah, the Most High, revealed: **'When the hypocrites come to you…'** (Surah Al-Munafiqun, 63:1). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then sent for me, recited it, and said: **'Indeed, Allah has confirmed your truthfulness.'**

Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: *This is a hasan sahih hadith.*"
3397,
3398,Hudhaifah bin Al-Yaman (ra) narrated that:When the Prophet (ﷺ) would sleep
3399,Al-Bara' bin `Azib (ra) narrated that:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to lay his head upon his right hand when going to sleep
3400,Abu Hurairah (ra) said:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to order that when one of us went to sleep
3401,Abu Hurairah (ra) narrated that :the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “When one of you leaves his bed then returns to it
3402,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Abd ibn Humayd narrated to us:** Abd ar-Razzaq informed us, from Ma'mar, from az-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Thawr, who said:

I heard **Ibn Abbas** (may Allah be pleased with both of them) say:

""I remained persistently eager to ask **Umar** (may Allah be pleased with him) about the two wives of the Prophet ﷺ concerning whom Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said:

> *'If you two turn in repentance to Allah, (you have cause to do so) for your hearts have deviated.'* (At-Tahrim, 66:4)

Until Umar performed the Hajj and I performed it with him. I poured water for him from the vessel so he could perform ablution, and I said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, who are the two wives of the Prophet ﷺ concerning whom Allah said: *""If you two turn in repentance to Allah, for your hearts have deviated, and if you two support one another against him, then indeed Allah is his Protector""* (At-Tahrim, 66:4)?'

He said to me: 'How astonishing of you, O Ibn Abbas!' Az-Zuhri said: 'And, by Allah, he disliked that he had been asked about it, yet he did not conceal it.' He said to me: 'They are **Aisha** and **Hafsa** (may Allah be pleased with both of them).'

Then he began narrating the hadith to me and said:

'We, the people of Quraysh, used to have authority over our women. When we came to Madinah, we found a people whose women had authority over them. Our women began learning from their women. One day I became angry with my wife, and she began to argue back with me. I disapproved of her arguing with me, and she said: ""What do you disapprove of? By Allah, the wives of the Prophet ﷺ do argue with him, and one of them would boycott him from day until night.""

I said to myself: ""She who does that among them has indeed failed and lost.""

My dwelling was in al-Awali among the Banu Umayyah, and I had a neighbor from the Ansar. We used to take turns descending to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — he would descend one day and bring me news of the Revelation and other matters, and I would descend one day and bring him the like of that.

We used to narrate that Ghassan were shoeing their horses to raid us.

One day he came to me at nightfall and knocked on my door. I went out to him and he said: 'A tremendous matter has occurred!' I said: 'Have Ghassan come?' He said: 'Even greater than that! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has divorced his wives!'

I said to myself: 'Hafsa has indeed failed and lost. I had been thinking that this would come to pass.'

When I prayed the Fajr prayer, I fastened my garments and went until I entered upon Hafsa. She was weeping. I said: 'Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divorced you?' She said: 'I do not know. He is secluded in this upper chamber.'

I went and came to a young black slave and said: 'Seek permission for Umar.' He entered and then came out to me and said: 'I have mentioned you to him, but he said nothing.'

I went to the mosque, and around the pulpit there were a group of people weeping. I sat with them, but what I felt overcame me, so I went to the young slave and said: 'Seek permission for Umar.' He entered and then came out to me and said: 'I have mentioned you to him, but he said nothing.'

I went again to the mosque and sat down, but what I felt overcame me, so I went to the young slave and said: 'Seek permission for Umar.' He entered and then came out to me and said: 'I have mentioned you to him, but he said nothing.'

I turned to leave, and the young slave called me and said: 'Enter, for permission has been granted to you.'

I entered and found the Prophet ﷺ reclining upon a woven mat of palm leaves. I could see its marks on his side. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, have you divorced your wives?' He said: **'No.'**

I said: 'Allah is the Greatest! O Messenger of Allah, we, the people of Quraysh, used to have authority over our women. When we came to Madinah, we found a people whose women had authority over them. Our women began learning from their women. One day I became angry with my wife, and she began to argue back with me. I disapproved of that, and she said: ""What do you disapprove of? By Allah, the wives of the Prophet ﷺ do argue with him, and one of them would boycott him from day until night.""'

I said: 'O Hafsa, do you argue with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' She said: 'Yes, and one of us would boycott him from day until night.'

I said: 'She who does that among you has indeed failed and lost. Does any one of you feel safe that Allah may become angry with her due to the anger of His Messenger, for she would have perished?'

The Prophet ﷺ smiled.

Then I said: 'O Hafsa, do not argue with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and do not ask him for anything. Ask me whatever you wish, and do not let it deceive you that your companion may be more beautiful than you and more beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'

He smiled again, and I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, may I speak privately?' He said: **'Yes.'**

I raised my head and saw nothing in the house except three items.

I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to expand the provision for your Ummah, for He has expanded it upon Persia and Rome, though they do not worship Him.'

He sat up straight and said: **'Are you in doubt, O son of al-Khattab? Those are a people whose good things have been hastened for them in the life of this world.'**

He had sworn that he would not go to his wives for a month. Allah reproached him for that and made the expiation of an oath incumbent upon him.""

Az-Zuhri said: **Urwah** informed me, from **Aisha** (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

""When twenty-nine days had passed, the Prophet ﷺ entered upon me, beginning with me, and said: **'O Aisha, I wish to mention something to you, so do not hasten until you consult your parents.'**

Then he recited this verse:

> *'O Prophet, say to your wives…'* (At-Tahrim, 66:25) — the verse.

She said: 'By Allah, my parents would never command me to leave him.' So I said: 'Should I consult my parents regarding this? Indeed I desire Allah, His Messenger, and the Hereafter.'""

Ma'mar said: **Ayyub** informed me that Aisha said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, do not inform your wives that I have chosen you."" The Prophet ﷺ said: **'Allah has sent me only as a conveyor; He has not sent me as one who causes hardship.'**

This is a **hasan sahih gharib** hadith. It has been narrated through more than one chain from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them)."
3403,Farwah bin Nawfal (ra) narrated that:He came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: “O Messenger of Allah
