3419,"Narrated by Yahya ibn Durust, a Basran scholar, who reported from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Hammad stated that this narration is, in our view, elevated (marfū') to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. The verse: ""The Day when mankind will stand before the Lord of the Worlds"" (Qur'an 83:6) — he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""They will stand in sweat up to the middle of their ears."""
3420,Abu Salamah said:“I asked Aishah [may Allah be pleased with her]: ‘With what did the Prophet used to open his Salat when he stood up in the night?’ She said: ‘When he stood up in the night
3421,Ali bin Abi Talib narrated that whenever the Messenger of Allah would stand for Salat
3422,Ali bin Abi Talib narrated that whenever the Messenger of Allah would stand for Salat
3423,Ali bin Abi Talib narrated that :when the Messenger of Allah would stand for the obligatory prayer
3424,Al-Hasan bin Muhammad bin Ubaidullah bin Abi Yazid said:“Ibn Juraij said to me: “Ubaidullah bin Abi Yazid informed me that Ibn Abbas said: “A man came to the Prophet and said: ‘O Messenger of Allah! I had a dream at night while I was sleeping
3425,Aishah narrated:“When the Messenger of Allah would prostrate (for recitation of) the Qur’an
3426,Anas bin Malik narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Whoever says – that is: when he leaves his house – ‘In the Name of Allah
3427,Umm Salamah narrated that :When the Prophet (ﷺ) would leave his house
3428,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah narrated from Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, from ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:**

""We were at a funeral procession in al-Baqīʿ when the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) came and sat down, and we sat with him. He had a stick with which he was tapping the ground. Then he raised his head toward the heavens and said:

**'There is no soul that has been breathed into except that its destination has already been written.'**

The people said, 'O Messenger of Allāh, shall we then rely upon our decree and abandon deeds?' He (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said:

**'Rather, perform deeds, for each one is facilitated. As for the one who is among the people of happiness, he will be facilitated toward the deeds of happiness. And as for the one who is among the people of wretchedness, he will be facilitated toward the deeds of wretchedness.'**

Then he recited:

*❝As for he who gives and fears Allāh, and believes in the best [reward], We will ease him toward ease. But as for he who withholds and considers himself free of need, and denies the best [reward], We will ease him toward difficulty.❞* (Sūrat al-Layl, 92:5–10)

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith.**"
3429,"# Translation of the Hadith

Narrated by Ibn Abi 'Umar, who narrated from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Jundub al-Bajali, who said:

""I was with the Prophet ﷺ in a cave when his finger bled. The Prophet ﷺ said: **'You are nothing but a finger that has bled, and whatever you have encountered has been in the cause of Allah.'**

He (Jundub) said: Jibrīl (Gabriel), peace be upon him, was delayed in coming to him (the Prophet ﷺ). The polytheists then said, 'Muhammad has been forsaken.' Thereupon, Allah, the Most High, revealed:

> *'Your Lord has not forsaken you, nor is He displeased.'*
> (Surah Ad-Duha, 93:3)

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a **Hasan Sahih** (good and authentic) Hadith. It has also been narrated by Shu'bah and al-Thawri from al-Aswad ibn Qays."
3430,Al-Agharr Abu Muslim narrated that he bears witness from Abu Sa’eed Al-Khudri and Abu Hurairah
3431,Umar narrated that:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whoever sees a person afflicted and say: ‘All praise is due to Allah Who saved me from that which He has afflicted you with
3432,Abu Hurairah narrated that:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whoever sees an afflicted person then says: ‘All praise is due to Allah who saved me from that which He has afflicted you with
3433,Abu Hurairah narrated that:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whoever sits in a sitting and engages in much empty
3434,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Mahmūd ibn Ghaylān** narrated to us, that **Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī** narrated to them, that **al-Qāsim ibn al-Faḍl al-Ḥaddānī** narrated from **Yūsuf ibn Saʿd**, who said:

A man stood before **al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī** (may Allah be pleased with him) after he had pledged allegiance to **Muʿāwiyah** (may Allah be pleased with him), and said: *""You have blackened the faces of the believers!""* — or he said: *""O you who have blackened the faces of the believers!""*

He (al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him) replied: *""Do not rebuke me, may Allah have mercy upon you! Indeed, the Prophet ﷺ was shown the Banū Umayyah upon his pulpit, and that distressed him. So Allah, the Most High, revealed:* ***""Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar""*** **(Sūrat al-Kawthar, 108:1)** — meaning, O Muḥammad, a river in Paradise — **And He revealed:** ***""Indeed, We sent it down on the Night of Decree. And what can make you know what the Night of Decree is? The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months""*** **(Sūrat al-Qadr, 97:1–3)** — *""The Banū Umayyah shall possess it (i.e., rule for its span) after you, O Muḥammad.""*

**Al-Qāsim** said: *""We counted them (the months), and lo, they amounted to one thousand months, neither increasing nor decreasing by a single day.""*

**Abū ʿĪsā** (al-Tirmidhī) said: *""This is a gharīb (uncommon/singular) hadith. We do not know it except from this chain, from the hadith of al-Qāsim ibn al-Faḍl. And it has also been narrated from al-Qāsim ibn al-Faḍl from Yūsuf ibn Māzin. Al-Qāsim ibn al-Faḍl al-Ḥaddānī is trustworthy (thiqah), and he was declared trustworthy by Yaḥyā ibn Saʿid and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī. As for Yūsuf ibn Saʿd, he is an unknown man (rajul majhūl). And we do not know this hadith in this wording except from this chain.""*

---

### Scholarly Notes:

- **Al-Kawthar**: A river in Paradise granted to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, as mentioned in Sūrat al-Kawthar (108:1).
- **The Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr)**: The Night of Power, described in Sūrat al-Qadr (97:1–3) as being better than a thousand months.
- **Gharīb**: A hadith that is singular in its chain of narration at a particular point, or unique in its wording/content.
- **Thiqah**: A grade of trustworthiness in Hadith narrator criticism, indicating a reliable and upright narrator.
- **Majhūl**: A narrator about whom insufficient information is available to independently verify his reliability.
- **Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī** (may Allah be pleased with him): The grandson of the Prophet ﷺ, who pledged allegiance to Muʿāwiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) in the year of al-Jamāʿah (41 AH), an event of great significance in early Islamic history."
3435,"Narrated by Ibn Abi 'Umar, narrated by Sufyan, from 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah and 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, who both heard Zirr ibn Hubaysh — and Zirr ibn Hubaysh was known as Abu Maryam — say: I said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b, ""Your brother 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud says: 'Whoever stands (in prayer) throughout the year will attain Laylat al-Qadr.'"" Ubayy said: ""May Allah forgive Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. He knows that it is in the last ten nights of Ramadan, and that it is the twenty-seventh night. However, he intended that the people should not become complacent (and rely solely on that)."" Then he swore, without exception, that it is the twenty-seventh night. I asked him: ""By what do you say that, O Abu al-Mundhir?"" He said: ""By the sign that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) informed us of — or by the sign — that the sun rises on that day without rays."" Abu 'Isa said: This is a hasan sahih hadith.

Narrated by Muhammad ibn al-'Ala', narrated by Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, who said: Abu Wa'il Shaqiq ibn Salamah would not speak as long as Zirr ibn Hubaysh was sitting. 'Asim ibn Bahdalah said: Zirr ibn Hubaysh was an eloquent man, and 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud used to ask him about the Arabic language.

Narrated by Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated by Yazid ibn Mihran al-Kufi, narrated by Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, who said: A man passed by Zirr ibn Hubaysh while he was giving the adhan and said: ""O Abu Maryam, are you giving the adhan? I would prefer you not to give the adhan."" Zirr said: ""You would prefer me not to give the adhan? By Allah, I will never speak to you again."""
3436,Abu Hurairah narrated that:When a matter would worry the Prophet (ﷺ)
3437,Sa`d bin Abi Waqqas narrated:From Khawlah bint Al Hakim As-Sulamiyyah
3438,"Mahmūd ibn Ghaylān narrated to us, saying: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated to us from Qatādah, from Muṭarrif ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr, from his father, that he came upon the Prophet"
3439,Abdullah bin Sarjis narrated that:When the Prophet (ﷺ) wanted to travel
3440,"# Hadith of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him)

**Narrator:** This tradition was related by al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him), from his father.

**Chain of Transmission:** Through the chain of narration — Ibn Abi 'Umar narrated to us; Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah related to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah, from Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, from al-Zubayr himself (may Allah be pleased with him).

**Text of the Narration:**

When the verse of the Qur'an was revealed: *""Then you will surely be asked that Day about the pleasures [you enjoyed]""* (Surah al-Takathur, 102:8), al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) asked: ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), which pleasures shall we be asked about? Surely, they are only these two humble things — dates and water.""

The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) replied: **""Indeed, it shall certainly come to pass.""**

**Classification:** This is a *hadith* classified as **hasan** (good/sound)."
3441,Anas narrated that:When the Prophet would return from a trip and see the walls of Al-Madinah
3442,"Abd ibn Humaid narrated to us, from Shabābah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿArzam al-Ashʿarī, who said: I heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Indeed, the first thing about which the servant will be questioned on the Day of Judgment regarding his blessings is that it will be said to him: 'Did We not make your body healthy and quench your thirst with cool water?'""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: This is a *gharīb* hadith. Al-Ḍaḥḥāk is Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿArzab, though it is also said ibn ʿArzam, and ibn ʿArzam is more correct."
