3469,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه:**

The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whenever he mentioned someone and supplicated for him, he would begin by supplicating for himself first.

**Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said:** This is a *hasan gharib sahih* hadith. And Abu Qatn's name is 'Amr ibn al-Haytham.

---

### Notes on Translation:

- **""بَدَأَ بِنَفْسِهِ""** — ""he would begin by supplicating for himself"" — This reflects the noble practice of the Prophet ﷺ to place himself first in supplication when praying for others, demonstrating humility and self-awareness of his own need for Allah's mercy.
- **""حَسَنٌ غَرِيبٌ"
3470,"Abū Mūsā, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, Ibrāhīm ibn Yaʿqūb, and others narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn ʿĪsā al-Juhaniyy narrated to us, from Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī Sufyān al-Jumaḥiyy, from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh, from his father, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) would, when he raised his hands in supplication, not lower them until he wiped his face with them.

Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā said in his narration: ""He did not return them until he wiped his face with them.""

Abū ʿĪsā said: This is a ṣaḥīḥ (authentic) and gharīb (uncommon) hadith. We do not know it except through the narration of Ḥammād ibn ʿĪsā, who narrated it uniquely. He is a narrator of few hadiths, and people have narrated from him. Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī Sufyān is trustworthy (thiqah), and Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān declared him trustworthy."
3471,Az-Zuhri said:“A Tasbihah in Ramadan is better than a thousand Tasbihah in other that it.”
3472,Tamim Ad-Dari narrated that:The Messsenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whoever says ten times: ‘I bear witness that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah. Alone
3473,Abu Dharr narrated that:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whoever says at the end of every Fajr prayer
3474,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Sufyān ibn Wakī' narrated to us**, saying: **Jarīr narrated to us**, from **al-Ḥasan ibn 'Ubayd Allāh**, from **Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd**, from **'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd**, from **'Abdullāh** (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:

> The Prophet ﷺ, when evening came upon him, would say:
>
> *""We have reached the evening, and the dominion belongs to Allāh, and all praise is due to Allāh. There is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh alone, without partner — I think he said therein: 'To Him belongs the dominion, and to Him belongs all praise, and He is over all things competent. I ask You for the good of this night and the good that follows it, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of this night and the evil that follows it. I seek refuge in You from laziness and the evil of old age. And I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the Fire and the punishment of the grave.'""*
>
> And when morning came upon him, he would also say:
>
> *""We have reached the morning, and the dominion belongs to Allāh, and all praise is due to Allāh.""*

He (the compiler) said: **This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith.** Shu'bah also narrated it through this chain from Ibn Mas'ūd, but he did not raise it (to the Prophet ﷺ).

---

**Source:** Jāmi' al-Tirmidhī"
