3506,
3507,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Khallāl narrated to us**, who said: **Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Hāshimī narrated to us**, who said: **'Abd ar-Rahmān ibn Abi'z-Zinād narrated to us**, from **Mūsā ibn 'Uqbah**, from **'Abdullāh ibn al-Fadl**, from **'Abd ar-Rahmān al-A'raj**, from **'Ubaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfi'**, from **'Alī ibn Abī Tālib** (may Allāh be pleased with him), from the **Messenger of Allāh** (peace and blessings be upon him), that:

---

## Raising the Hands at Specific Points in Prayer

When he (peace and blessings be upon him) stood for the **obligatory prayer (al-salāt al-maktūbah)**, he would raise his hands to the level of his shoulders. He would also do this **when he had completed his recitation and intended to bow into rukū'**, and he would do it **when he raised his head from rukū'**. He would **not** raise his hands during any part of his prayer while he was **seated**. And when he **stood up from the two prostrations (sajdatayn)**, he would likewise raise his hands and say the takbīr.

---

## The Opening Supplication (Du'ā' al-Iftitāh)

When he opened the prayer, after the takbīr, he would say:

> *""I have turned my face toward Him Who created the heavens and the earth, as a man of pure faith, and I am not of the polytheists (mushrikīn). Indeed, my prayer, my sacrifice, my living, and my dying are for Allāh, the Lord of all the worlds. He has no partner, and with that I have been commanded, and I am of the Muslims.*
>
> *O Allāh, You is the Sovereign (al-Malik). There is no god but You. Glorified are You! You is my Lord, and I am Your servant. I have wronged myself, and I acknowledge my sin, so forgive me all my sins, for none forgives sins except You. Guide me to the best of character, for none guides to the best of it except You. Turn away from me the evil of it, for none turns away its evil except You. Here I am (labbayka) and at Your service (sa'dayka), and I am with You and toward You. There is no escape from You and no refuge except with You. I seek Your forgiveness and I repent unto You.""*

---

## Supplication During Rukū' (Bowing)

Then he would recite. And when he bowed into rukū', his speech during his rukū' would be to say:

> *""O Allāh, to You I have bowed, and in You I have believed, and to You I have submitted, and You is my Lord. My hearing, my sight, my marrow, and my bones are humbled before Allāh, the Lord of all the worlds.""*

---

## Supplication Upon Raising the Head from Rukū'

And when he raised his head from rukū', he would say:

> *""Allāh hears the one who praises Him (Sami' Allāhu liman hamidah).""*

Then he would follow it with:

> *""O Allāh, our Lord, to You is all praise — praise filling the heavens and the earth, and filling whatever You will beyond that.""*

---

## Supplication During Sujūd (Prostration)

And when he prostrated, he would say in his sujūd:

> *""O Allāh, to You I have prostrated, and in You I have believed, and to You I have submitted, and You is my Lord. My face has prostrated before Him Who created it and fashioned its hearing and its sight. Blessed is Allāh, the Best of Creators (ahsan al-khāliqīn).""*

---

## Supplication Upon Concluding the Prayer

And he would say **when he turned away from the prayer (upon its completion)**:

> *""O Allāh, forgive me for what I have sent forth and what I have left behind, what I have concealed and what I have made public. You is my God (ilāhī); there is no god but You.""*

---

## Scholarly Assessment

**Abū 'Īsā (at-Tirmidhī, may Allāh have mercy on him)** said: **""This is a hasan sahīh hadith.""**

And **the practice (al-'amal) according to this** is held by **ash-Shāfi'ī** (may Allāh have mercy on him) and **some of our companions**.

**Abū 'Īsā** said: **""And Ahmad (ibn Hanbal, may Allāh have mercy on him) did not hold this view.""**

And **some of the people of knowledge (ahl al-'ilm)**, among the **people of Kūfah and others**, said: **""This (opening supplication) is said in the voluntary prayer (salāt at-tatawwu'), and it is not said in the obligatory prayer (al-maktūbah).""**

I heard **Abū Ismā'īl at-Tirmidhī, Muhammad ibn Ismā'īl ibn Yūsuf**, say: **""I heard Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Hāshimī say — and he mentioned this hadith — and he said: 'This, according to us, is like the hadith of az-Zuhrī from Sālim from his father.'""**

---

*And Allāh, the Most High, knows best.*"
3508,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“When you pass by the gardens of Paradise
3509,Anas bin Malik [may Allah be pleased with him] narrated that the Messenger of Allah said:“When you pass by the gardens of Paradise
3510,"# Hadith Translation

**Narrated to us Sa'īd ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd al-Umawī:** My father narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayy, from Isḥāq ibn 'Abdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allāh be pleased with him), who reported that the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa sallam) said:

> *""Whoever says — meaning when he exits his home — 'Bismillāh, I place my trust in Allāh; there is no power and no strength except with Allāh' (بِسْمِ اللَّهِ تَوَكَّلْتُ عَلَى اللَّهِ لَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ), it is said to him: 'You have been sufficed and protected (kufīta wa wuqīta),' and the devil withdraws from him (wa tanāḥā 'anhu ash-shayṭān).""*

**Abū 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** *""This is a ḥasan, sahīh, gharīb hadith. We do not know it except from this chain of narration (wajh).""*

---

**Transliteration of the dhikr:**
*Bismillāh, tawakkaltu 'alallāh, lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh.*"
3511,Anas bin Malik narrated that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said:“O Messenger of Allah
3512,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ahmad ibn Mani'** — **Yazid ibn Harun** narrated to us — **Azhar ibn Sinan** informed us — **Muhammad ibn Wasi'** narrated to us, who said:

""I came to Makkah, and my brother **Salim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar** met me and narrated to me from his father, from his grandfather, that the **Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him**, said:

> *'Whoever enters the marketplace and says: ""There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone, without partner. To Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs all praise. He gives life and causes death, and He is Ever-Living and does not die. In His hand is all good, and He is over all things competent"" — Allah will record for him a million million good deeds, erase from him a million million sins, and raise him by a million million degrees.'*

**Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi)** said: *""This is a Gharīb (uncommon) Hadith.""*

He further noted: **'Amr ibn Dinar** — who was the steward (*qahramān*) of the family of al-Zubayr — also narrated this Hadith from **Salim ibn 'Abdullah** in a similar manner.

---

*Note: This Hadith is classified as Gharīb by Imam al-Tirmidhi, meaning it has a limited chain of narration. The reward described — a million million (one trillion) good deeds, erasure of sins, and elevation of degrees — underscores the immense virtue of remembering Allah, even in the mundane setting of a marketplace.*"
3513,Al-`Abbas bin `Abdul-Muttalib said:“I said: ‘O Messenger of Allah
3514,Ibn Umar narrated that the Messenger of Allah said (ﷺ):“Allah has not been asked for anything more beloved to Him than being asked for Al-`Āfiyah
3515,"# Hadith Translation

**Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bazī' — narrated to us 'Abdul-Wārith ibn Sa'īd — from 'Amr ibn Dīnār, the mawlā of the family of al-Zubayr — from Sālim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar — from Ibn 'Umar — from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:**

""Whoever sees one afflicted with a trial and says, *'Al-hamdu lillāhi alladhī 'āfānī mimmā abtālāka bihi wa faḍḍalanī 'alā kathīrimimman khalaqa tafḍīlā'* (All praise is due to Allah, Who has spared me from that with which you have been tested, and has favoured me over many of those whom He has created), will be spared that trial, whatever it may be, for as long as he lives.""

---

**Abu 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** This is a gharīb (uncommon/singular) hadith. There are narrations on this topic from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). 'Amr ibn Dīnār, the qahramān (steward) of the family of al-Zubayr, is a Basran shaykh who is not considered strong (qawī) in hadith. He has reported singular narrations from Sālim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar. It has been narrated from Abū Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Alī that he said: When one sees a person afflicted with a trial, one should seek refuge from it (inwardly) and say this to oneself, without letting the afflicted person hear it.

---

*Note: This hadith is recorded in the collection of al-Tirmidhī, who graded it as gharīb. The supplication mentioned is a prescribed form of gratitude to Allah, Exalted is He, for being spared from another's affliction, and it is recommended to be said quietly so as not to cause distress to the afflicted person.*"
3516,Abu Malik Al-Ash`ari narrated that the Messenger of Allah said:“Al-Wudu is half of faith
3517,Abdullah bin Amr narrated that the Messenger of Allah said:“At-Tasbīḥ is half of the Scale
3518,A man from Banu Sulaim narrated:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) counted them out in my hand” - or - “in his hand: ‘At-Tasbīḥ is half of the Scale
3519,Ali bin Abi Talib said:“The most of what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) supplicated with during the afternoon at Arafat while standing was: ‘O Allah to You is the praise like the one You say
3520,Abu Umamah narrated:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) supplicated with many supplications of which we did not preserve a thing. We said: ‘O Messenger of Allah
3521,Shahr bin Hawshab said:“I said to Umm Salamah: ‘O Mother of the Believers! What was the supplication that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said most frequently when he was with you?” She said: ‘The supplication he said most frequently was: “O Changer of the hearts
3522,Sulaiman bin Buraidah narrated that his father said:“Khalid bin Al-Walid al Makhzumi complained to the Prophet (ﷺ) saying: ‘O Messenger of Allah
3523,"Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah al-Dhabbiyy, narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from 'Abdullah ibn Sarjis, who said:

When the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, would travel, he would say:

""O Allah, You are the Companion in the journey and the Guardian over the family. O Allah, accompany us in our journey and watch over our family in our absence. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel, from a sorrowful return, from apostasy after faithfulness, from the supplication of the oppressed, and from an evil sight upon one's family and wealth.""

He said: This is a hasan sahih hadith.

He said: It is also narrated as ""al-hawr ba'da al-kawr"" (apostasy after faithfulness). He said: The meaning of his statement ""al-hawr ba'da al-kawn"" or ""al-kawr"" — both are valid — is the return from faith to disbelief, or from obedience to sin. It refers to a return from one state to another state of evil."
3524,Anas narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:“Be constant with: ‘O Possessor of Majesty and Honor (Yā Dhal-Jalāli wal-Ikrām).’”
3525,Abu Umamah Al-Bahili said:“I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: “Whoever goes to his bed while in a state of purity and remembering Allah
3526,Mu`adh bin Jabal narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) heard a man supplicating
3527,"Narrated to us Ismāʿīl ibn Mūsā al-Fazārī, narrated to us Saʿīd ibn Khuthaym, from Ḥanẓalah, from Sālim, that Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to say to a man when he intended to travel: ""Come close to me so that I may bid you farewell, just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to bid us farewell."" He would then say: ""I entrust to Allah your religion, your trustworthiness, and the final deeds of your life."" He (the narrator) said: ""This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīh gharīb hadith from this chain, from the hadith of Sālim ibn ʿAbdillāh."""
3528,Abu Rashid Al-Hubrani said:“I came to `Abdullah bin `Amr and said to him: ‘Report something to me that you heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).’ So he set forth before me a scroll and said: ‘This is what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) wrote for me.’” He said: “So I looked in it and found in it: ‘Indeed
3529,Amr bin Murrah said:“I heard Abu Wa’il say: ‘`Abdullah bin Mas`ud said’ and I said to him: ‘Did you hear it from `Abdullah?’ He said: ‘Yes.’ And he narrated it in Marfu` form that he said: ‘There is none with more Ghirah than Allah
3530,Abdullah bin `Amr narrated from Abu Bakr As-Siddiq that he said:“O Messenger of Allah
3531,Al-Muttalib bin Abi Wada`ah said:“Al-`Abbas came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and it is as if he heard something
3532,Anas bin Malik narrated that:the Prophet (ﷺ) passed by a tree with dry leaves
3533,Umarah bin Shabib As-Saba’i narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Whoever says: none has the right to be worshipped but Allah
3534,Zirr bin Hubaish said:“I came to Safwan bin `Assal Al Muradi to ask him about wiping over the Khuff
3535,Zirr bin Hubaish said:“I came to Safwan bin `Assal Al-Muradi so he said to me: ‘What has brought you
3536,Ibn `Umar narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:“Indeed Allah accepts the repentance of a slave as long as (his soul does not reach his throat).”
3537,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Allah is more delighted with the repentance of one of you
3538,"Narrated by al-Ansari, narrated by Ma'n, narrated by Malik, and reported by Qutaibah, from Malik, from Suhail ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""When the people saw the first fruits, they would bring them to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) took it, he would say:

'O Allah, bless us in our fruits, and bless us in our city, and bless us in our sa' and our mudd. O Allah, indeed Ibrahim is Your servant, Your intimate friend, and Your prophet, and I am Your servant and Your prophet. He prayed to You for Makkah, and I pray to You for Madinah with the same as he prayed to You for Makkah, and the same again with it.'

Then he would call the smallest child he saw and give him that fruit.""

He said: ""This is a hasan sahih hadith."""
3539,Anas bin Malik narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Allah
3540,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Allah created a hundred mercies
3541,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“If the believer knew what is with Allah of punishment
3542,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Verily
3543,Anas said that:The Prophet (ﷺ) entered the Masjid and there was a man who had performed Salat and was supplicating. He was saying in his supplication: “O Allah
3544,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“May the man before whom I am mentioned - and he does not send Salat upon me - be humiliated. And may a man upon whom Ramadan enters and then passes
3545,Ali bin Abi Talib narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“The stingy person is the one before whom I am mentioned
3546,Abdullah bin [Abi] Awfa narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say:“O Allah
3547,Ibn `Umar narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Whomsoever of you the door of supplication is opened for
3548,That was narrated to us by Al-Qasim bin Dinar Al-Kufi (who said):“Ishaq bin Mansur narrated to us
3549,"# Hadith Translation

**Jābir (may Allāh be pleased with him) reported that the Prophet Muḥammad (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said:**

**""Whoever says 'Subḥānallāhi l-ʿAẓīm wa bi-ḥamdihi' (Glorified is Allāh, the Magnificent, and with His praise), a palm tree will be planted for him in Paradise.""**

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** *""This is a ḥasan gharīb (good and singular) hadith.""*

---

**Transmitted by:** Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ → al‑Mu’ammil → Ḥammād ibn Salama → Abū al‑Zubayr → Jābir (may Allāh be pleased with him) → from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam).

**Note:** Abū ʿĪsā al‑Tirmidhī (raḥimahullāh) classified this narration as *ḥasan gharīb*, indicating its chain is good but singular in its route of transmission."
3550,"It was narrated to us Nasr ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman al-Kūfī, that al-Muhāribī narrated to them, from Mālik ibn Anas, from Sumayy, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said:

""Whoever says ‘Subḥān Allāh wa bi-ḥamdihi’ (Glorified is Allāh, and with His praise) one hundred times, his sins will be forgiven even if they were like the foam of the sea.""

Abū ‘Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith."
3551,"Narrated by Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allāh, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""Two words which are light upon the tongue, heavy upon the Scale, and beloved to the Most Merciful: *Subḥān Allāhi wa biḥamdihi, Subḥān Allāhil-ʿAẓīm* (Glory be to Allāh and His is the praise; Glory be to Allāh, the Magnificent).""

He (the compiler) said: This is a ḥasan gharīb ṣaḥīḥ hadīth."
3552,Abu Ayyub Al-Ansari narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Whoever says ten times: ‘None has the right to be worshipped by Allah
3553,Saffiyah narrated:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered upon me and before me were four thousand date pits
3554,"Narrated Isma'il ibn Musa al-Kufi, from Dawud ibn al-Zibriqan, from Matar al-Warraq, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said one day to his companions: ""Say 'Subhan Allah wa bihamdihi' (Glory be to Allah and His is the praise) one hundred times. Whoever says it once shall have ten good deeds written for him; whoever says it ten times shall have one hundred written for him; and whoever says it one hundred times shall have one thousand written for him. And whoever increases beyond that, Allah shall increase him further. And whoever seeks forgiveness, Allah shall forgive him.""

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a hasan gharib hadith."
3555,"Muḥammad ibn Wazīr al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, Abū Sufyān al-Ḥimyarī, who is Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā al-Wāsiṭī, narrated to us, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Ḥumrah, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said:

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""Whoever glorifies Allah (says *SubḥānAllāh*) one hundred times in the morning and one hundred times in the evening, it will be as if he performed Hajj one hundred times. And whoever praises Allah (says *Alḥamdulillāh*) one hundred times in the morning and one hundred times in the evening, it will be as if he provided one hundred horses for the cause of Allah—or he said: 'fought in one hundred military expeditions.' And whoever declares the oneness of Allah (says *Lā ilāha illAllāh*) one hundred times in the morning and one hundred times in the evening, it will be as if he freed one hundred slaves from the descendants of Ismāʿīl. And whoever magnifies Allah (says *Allāhu Akbar*) one hundred times in the morning and one hundred times in the evening, no one will come on that day with more than what he brought, except one who said the same as he said or added to what he said.""

Abū ʿĪsā said: This is a *ḥasan gharīb* hadith."
3556,Abu Hurairah narrated that:A man was supplicating with his two fingers so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Make it one
3557,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Khalīl ibn Murrah, from al-Azhar ibn 'Abdillāh, from Tamīm al-Dārī, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said:**

""Whoever says ten times: *'I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone, without partner — the One God, the Eternal, Absolute; He begets not, nor is He begotten, and there is none comparable unto Him'* — Allah will record for him forty thousand thousand (i.e., forty billion) good deeds.""

**It was said:** ""This is a *gharīb* (uncommon) Hadith; we do not know it except through this chain of narration. And al-Khalīl ibn Murrah is not considered strong (*qawī*) among the scholars of Hadith.""

**Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl** (i.e., Imām al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him) **said:** ""He is *munkar al-ḥadīth* (one whose narrations are rejected).""

---

### Notes on the Translation

- **"
3558,Abu Bakr narrated:That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “He who seeks forgiveness has not been persistent in sin
3559,Abu Umamah narrated:That `Umar bin Al-Khattab [may Allah be pleased with him] wore a new garment and said: “All praise is due to Allah who clothed me with what I may cover my `Awrah
3560,Umar bin Al-Khattab narrated:That the Prophet (ﷺ) sent an expedition in the direction of Najd. They gained many spoils of war and returned quickly. A man among those who did not go out said: “We have not seen an expedition quicker in return or greater in spoils than this expedition.” So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Should I not direct you to a group greater in spoils and quicker in return? A group who attended Salat As-Subh
3561,Ibn `Umar narrated from `Umar:that he sought permission from the Prophet (ﷺ) concerning `Umrah
3562,Ali [may Allah be pleased with him] narrated:that a Mukatib came to him and said: “Indeed I am not capable of my Kitabah so aid me.” He said: “Should I not teach you words that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) taught me? If you had a debt upon you similar to the mountain of Sir
3563,Ali said:“I was ill and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by me while I was saying: ‘O Allah
3564,"Abū Kurayb narrated to us, Muʿāwiyah ibn Hishām narrated to us, from Ḥamzah al-Zayyāt, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say: ""O Allāh, grant me health in my body and grant me health in my eyesight, and make it (my eyesight) the inheritor from me. There is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh, the Forbearing, the Generous. Glory be to Allāh, the Lord of the Magnificent Throne, and all praise is due to Allāh, the Lord of the worlds.""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: This is a ḥasan gharīb hadīth. He said: I heard Muḥammad (i.e., al-Bukhārī) say: Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit did not hear anything from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr. And Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit is Ḥabīb ibn Qays ibn Dīnār, and he did meet ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both). And Allāh knows best."
3565,Ali bin Abi Talib narrated:that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to say in his Witr: “O Allah
3566,Mus`ab bin Sa`d and `Amr bin Maimun narrated:“Sa`d used to teach his children these words just as the Muktib teaches children
3567,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him):**

He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to my father, ""O Husayn, how many gods do you worship today?"" My father replied, ""Seven — six on the earth and one in the heavens."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) asked, ""Which of them do you turn to for your hopes and your fears?"" He answered, ""The one in the heavens."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) then said, ""O Husayn, were you to embrace Islam, I would teach you two words that would benefit you.""

When Husayn later embraced Islam, he said, ""O Messenger of Allah, teach me the two words you promised me."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

**""Say: 'O Allah, inspire me with right guidance and protect me from the evil of my own self.'**
*(Allahumma alhimnī rushdī, wa a'idhnī min sharri nafsī.)*""

---

**Grading:** This is a *hasan gharib* (good and singular) hadith. It has also been narrated from Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) through other chains of transmission."
3568,Az-Zubair bin `Awwam narrated:that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: “There is not a morning that the slave of Allah reached except that a caller calls out: ‘Glorify the King
3569,"Narrated by Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to supplicate, saying:

""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from laziness, from senility, from cowardice, from miserliness, from the trial of the Dajjal (the False Messiah), and from the torment of the grave.""

Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a hasan sahih hadith."
3570,Abdullah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Ask Allah of His Bounty. For verily
3571,Zaid bin Arqam [may Allah be pleased with him] said that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to say:“O Allah
3572,Jubair bin Nufair narrated that `Ubadah bin As-Samit narrated to them that
3573,Al-Bara’ bin `Azib narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:“When you go to your bed
3574,"Abū Kurayb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us — from Muḥammad ibn Saʿd al-Anṣārī — from ʿAbdullāh ibn Rabīʿah al-Dimashqī — ʿĀʾidhullāh, Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī, narrated to us — from Abū al-Dardāʾ, who said:

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""It was from the supplication of Dāwūd, peace be upon him, that he would say: 'O Allah, I ask You for Your love, the love of those who love You, and the deed that will lead me to Your love. O Allah, make Your love more beloved to me than myself, my family, and cold water.'""

He (Abū al-Dardāʾ) said: ""And whenever the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, mentioned Dāwūd, peace be upon him, he would narrate about him and say: 'He was the most worshipful of mankind.'""

He (the compiler) said: ""This is a ḥasan gharīb hadith."""
3575,Abdullah bin Busr narrated:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stayed with my father.” So he said: “We brought some food near him
3576,Bilal bin Yasar bin Zaid [the freed slave of the Prophet(ﷺ)] narrated:“My father narrated to me
3577,Uthman bin Hunaif narrated that a blind man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said:“Supplicate to Allah to heal me.” He (ﷺ) said: “If you wish I will supplicate for you
3578,Abu Umamah [may Allah be pleased with him] said:`Amr bin `Abasah reported to me that he heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: “The closest that the Lord is to a worshipper is during the last part of the night
3579,Umarah bin Za`karah said:“I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying; ‘Indeed
3580,"Narrated to us was Harun bin Ishaq, narrated to us Abdah, from Hisham bin Urwah, from Abbad bin Abdullah bin al-Zubair, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saying at the time of his death: ""O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, and join me with the Highest Companion."" He said: This is a hasan sahih hadith."
3581,Safwan bin Sulaim said:“No angel has risen from the earth until he said: ‘There is no might or power except with Allah (Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh).’”
3582,Humaidah bint Yasir narrated from her grandmother Yusairah - and she was one of those who emigrated - she said:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to us: ‘Hold fast to At-Tasbih
3583,Anas narrated that when the Prophet (ﷺ) would go out for an expedition
3584,"# Hadith Translation

## Ḥadīth No. — Narrated by Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him)

**Narrated by 'Alī ibn Ḥujr — from 'Abd al-Ḥamd ibn 'Umar al-Hilālī — from Sa'īd ibn Iyās al-Jurayrī — from Abū al-Salīl — from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him):**

A man said: ""O Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, I heard your supplication tonight, and that which reached me of it was that you were saying: **'Allāhumma'ghfir lī dhanbī, wa wassi' lī fī rizqī, wa bārik lī fīmā razaqtanī'** (O Allāh, forgive my sin, expand my provision, and bless me in what You have provided for me).""

He ﷺ said: **""Do you see that they [the angels] have left anything out?""**

---

**Note:** This is a *gharīb* (uncommon/singular) ḥadīth.

Abū al-Salīl's name is Ḍurayb ibn Nufayr, though it is also said to be ibn Nuqayr.

---

*Ṣallā Allāhu 'alayhi wa sallam.*"
3585,Umar bin Al-Khattab said:“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) taught me
3586,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him):**

It was seldom that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would rise from a gathering without supplicating with these words for his Companions (may Allah be pleased with them):

> ""O Allah, apportion for us such fear of You as shall stand as a barrier between us and acts of disobedience to You; and such obedience to You as shall lead us to Your Paradise; and such certainty (yaqīn) as shall make the calamities of this world easy for us. Grant us the enjoyment of our hearing, our sight, and our strength for as long as You keep us alive, and make it (i.e., these faculties) an inheritor from us (i.e., let them remain with us until death). Avenge us upon the one who wrongs us, and grant us victory over the one who shows enmity toward us. Do not make our affliction be in our religion, and do not make this world the greatest of our concerns nor the utmost limit of our knowledge. And do not place in authority over us one who does not show us mercy.""

**Abu 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** This is a *hasan gharīb* hadith. Some of them have narrated this hadith from Khālid ibn Abī 'Imrān, from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
3587,Muhammad bin Sulaim narrated:“Thabit Al-Bunani narrated to me
3588,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by ʿAlī ibn Khashram**, who said: **al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā** informed us, from **al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid**, from **Abū Isḥāq**, from **al-Ḥārith**, from **ʿAlī** (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-sallam) said to me: **""Shall I not teach you words which, if you say them, Allāh will forgive you — even if you have already been forgiven?""**

He (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-sallam) said: **""Say: 'There is no god but Allāh, the Most High, the Most Great (Lā ilāha illallāhu al-ʿAliyyu al-ʿAẓīm); there is no god but Allāh, the Forbearing, the Generous (Lā ilāha illallāhu al-Ḥalīmu al-Karīm); there is no god but Allāh — Glory be to Allāh, the Lord of the Mighty Throne (Lā ilāha illallāh, Subḥānallāhi Rabbi al-ʿArshi al-ʿAẓīm).'""**

ʿAlī ibn Khashram said: And **ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid** informed us from his father with the same narration, except that he added at the end: **""All praise is due to Allāh, the Lord of all the worlds (Al-ḥamdu lillāhi Rabbil-ʿĀlamīn).""**

He (the compiler) said: **""This is a Gharīb (uncommon) Hadith. We do not know it except through this chain, from the Hadith of Abū Isḥāq from al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him).""**

---

*Note: The compiler classifies this narration as Gharīb, meaning it is reported through a single chain of transmission, and its chain includes al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar, who is considered weak (ḍaʿīf) by many Hadith scholars.*"
3589,Abu Hurairah [may Allah be pleased with him] narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“No worshipper has ever said: None has the right to be worshipped but Allah (Lā ilāha illallāh)’ sincerely
3590,"Narrated by Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said:

""Indeed, Allāh has ninety-nine names, one less than a hundred. Whoever enumerates them shall enter Paradise.""

Yūsuf said: And it was narrated to us by ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, from Muḥammad, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).

This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadīth. It has been narrated through multiple chains from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."
3591,Ibn `Umar [may Allah be pleased with him] said:“We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when a man among the people said: ‘Allah is most exceedingly great
3592,Abu Dharr narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) visited him
3593,Anas bin Malik narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“The supplication is not rejected between the Adhan and the Iqamah.” They said: “So what should we say
3594,Anas [bin Malik] narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:“The supplication is not rejected between the Adhan and the Iqamah.”
3595,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“The Mufarridun have preceded.” They said: “And who are the Mufarridun
3596,Abu Hurairah [may Allah be pleased with him] narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“That I should say: ‘Glory is to Allah (Subḥān Allāh).’ ‘All praise is due to Allah (Al-ḥamdulillāh)
3597,Abu Hurairah narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“There are three whose supplication is not rejected: The fasting person when he breaks his fast
3598,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by al-Tirmidhi:**

Ahmad ibn Mu'ayyad narrated to us, 'Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), teach me something that I may ask Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.' He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: **'Ask Allah for well-being (al-'afiyah).'**

I remained for several days, then I came and said: 'O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), teach me something that I may ask Allah.' So he (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me: **'O 'Abbas, O uncle of the Messenger of Allah, ask Allah for well-being (al-'afiyah) in this world and in the Hereafter.'**

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: **This is a sahih (authentic) hadith.** And 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal did hear (narrations) from al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him).

---

### Notes on Translation:

- **"
3599,Abu Hurairah or Abu Sa`eed Al-Khudri narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:“Indeed
